| 1 | 6 | Together in space and time. *How do dancers share space, hit the beat in threes and fours, and use muscle tension to make movement say something?* | 18 | da-g6-elements; da-g6-technique | DA:Pr4.1.6a; DA:Pr4.1.6b; DA:Pr4.1.6c; DA:Pr5.1.6a; DA:Pr5.1.6b; DA:Pr5.1.6c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding through the dances studied (review); creating through the partner and group sections the class builds. Works: stepping (United States, grown through the 1900s in the historically Black fraternities and sororities of colleges and universities, danced today), stamps, claps and slaps with accents in four, a piece by a named step team the unit plan picks, described and linked to a museum's or the team's own page; Johann Strauss II, The Blue Danube (Austria, 1867, public domain music), danced by the class as a waltz in three; a butoh work by Sankai Juku the unit plan picks (Japan, company founded in 1975 by Ushio Amagatsu; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), slow movement driven by muscle tension, described and linked to the company's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 5 floor and air paths with tight and flowing contrast (da-g5-elements) and a run of phrases with safe habits (da-g5-technique), each with its ten-minute reteach. DA:Pr4.1.6a: build partner and group sections by judging distance, varying paths and holding the eyes' focus near or far as planned. DA:Pr4.1.6b: mix sudden and sustained timing and land accents on the right beats in three-beat (the waltz) and four-beat (stepping) measures, counted aloud. DA:Pr4.1.6c: start movement from muscle tension to show dynamics, and tell bound from free-flowing movement and use each (butoh's held tension beside the waltz's flow). DA:Pr5.1.6a: direction changes, small jumps with soft landings, and clean transitions between phrases. DA:Pr5.1.6b: a safe warm-up built from basic anatomy (the big muscle groups and joints each move wakes), body signals (breath, heat, a pulled or sharp feeling means stop), space awareness, and food and water choices before a rehearsal for an invented dancer. DA:Pr5.1.6c: rehearse an ensemble section, spot what works, test fixes for a problem and track the group's growth on the rehearsal log. Words: formation, focus, measure, accent, bound, transition. Showing: group sections for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Pr4.1.6a, a guide look-for on the showing: partner and group spacing kept through a path change, eyes' focus near or far as planned; DA:Pr4.1.6b, a guide look-for on the showing: accents on the right beats in the three-beat and the four-beat section, with one sudden and one sustained change; DA:Pr4.1.6c, a show item at the teacher's spot: one short phrase danced bound then free-flowing, started from tension, each named; DA:Pr5.1.6a, a guide look-for on the showing: a direction change, a small jump landed softly and a clean transition; DA:Pr5.1.6b, a written item on the written day: a warm-up plan card for an invented dancer (three moves and the body part each wakes, one body signal to stop for, one space check, one food or water choice before rehearsal); DA:Pr5.1.6c, the kept rehearsal log from the last rehearsal days (what worked, one problem, the fix tested, the change since the baseline), scored after the session. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: separate tempos in separate body parts (grade 8), syncopation (Level 1). Tools: the elements card, the counts card for three and four, a warm-up step card, the rehearsal log, work cards, all Curio Class's own; a beat keeper or the printed counts card. Free path: a drum or a pot, claps, floor tape or chalk. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First because every later unit needs the floor, the warm-up, the stop cue, partner spacing and the rehearsal log (the dance-warm-up, phrase-recall and rehearsal-goals models return here first, where they are first used this year). Longest unit of the year: six ids, three technique steps (jumps and landings, the anatomy warm-up, ensemble fixes) that need fluency days, and the year's routines set up. Physical education's dance sequences (its unit 6, grade 6 weeks 22 to 27) come later in the year, so this unit uses moves students already have | Candidate: a waltz or another three-beat social dance from outside Europe beside Strauss, for range | Hazards and controls: jumps small and landed through the feet with soft knees, on wood or mats, never on concrete or tile; step stamps in soft shoes or bare feet, slaps on the thighs and arms light; partner work keeps arm's length unless both agree to contact; no lifts; floor checked first; water and rest offered. Stepping: it grew in Black fraternities and sororities and carries their history and pride; the sensitivity agent reads a museum's page and a step team's or organization's own published words first, the class dances step patterns the unit plan writes, and no student copies an organization's own calls, chants or signs. Butoh: some works carry postwar themes; the unit plan picks a piece without them, and no student wears body paint. The invented dancer carries every food and water choice; no student is asked about their own eating or body. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | 6 | Ideas that agree and ideas that clash. *How can two ideas that agree or clash, tried in different styles, become a dance study with a goal?* | 14 | da-g6-ideas; da-g6-form | DA:Cr1.1.6a; DA:Cr1.1.6b; DA:Cr2.1.6a; DA:Cr2.1.6b | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: Lucinda Childs, Dance (United States, 1979; Childs born 1940, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), made with Philip Glass's music and Sol LeWitt's film of the dancers, three arts joined, described and linked to the company's page; Jerome Robbins, Fancy Free (United States, 1944; Robbins died 1998), ballet mixed with the social dances of its day, its three sailors' solos only, described and linked to New York City Ballet's or a dance archive's page: one maker joins ideas from other arts, the other joins styles | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 5 material from several sources (da-g5-ideas) and several tools altered together (da-g5-form), each with its reteach. DA:Cr1.1.6a: join ideas from two different inspirations (a picture and a sound, a poem line and a place, a nature process and a chance roll) by what they share or how they clash, on the inspiration list. DA:Cr1.1.6b: try one idea in three styles from the style menu (ballet, modern, jazz, hip-hop, a social dance the class knows) and see how each changes the steps. DA:Cr2.1.6a: build a study of about 32 counts with choreographic tools (repetition, canon, retrograde, accumulation) and a form (AB, ABA or rondo), its goal stated in one line before it is danced. DA:Cr2.1.6b: write three criteria for a personal or cultural study (an invented dancer's or a public figure's option marked the same) and judge which of two versions of a move says it best. Words: inspiration, contrast, style, canon, form, criterion. Showing: the studies shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Cr1.1.6a, the kept inspiration list: two ideas, the joining word (alike or opposite) and the moves taken from each; DA:Cr1.1.6b, a written item on the written day: the styles note, three styles tried on one idea and one feature of each that changed the steps; DA:Cr2.1.6a, a guide look-for on the showing: at least two tools and the form seen, and the goal stated before dancing; DA:Cr2.1.6b, the kept criteria sheet: three criteria and the judged pair of moves with a reason from the criteria. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: sources compared and styles chosen for an aim (grade 7). Tools: the inspiration list, style menu, tools card and criteria sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: picture and sound cards printed, a die for the chance roll. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 1 because a study needs the spacing, accents and tension just taught as its material; before unit 3 because unit 3 revises this study against group criteria. The style-menu model starts here, where it is first needed | Candidate: a study inspiration from a maker outside the United States beside Childs and Robbins | Personal studies are invited, never required: the invented dancer's or public figure's option is offered beside them and marked the same; a cultural study uses a public tradition from a source, never a classmate's family. Fancy Free's sailors flirt and scuffle; the unit uses the three solos only. Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: canon and traveling in groups keep the marked lanes; the stop cue; floor checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
