ilies, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Pr6.1.7a, a guide look-for on the family showing (a space adaptation used, etiquette kept, a note applied), with the kept rehearsal journal; DA:Pr6.1.7b, the kept venue card: one other venue or audience and two production elements that would change, with reasons; DA:Cn10.1.7a, a written item on the written day: two styles' qualities contrasted with the student's or the invented dancer's, and the perspective each shows; DA:Cn10.1.7b, a guide look-for on the spirit study with the kept research card (two sources, the history in three lines); DA:Cn11.1.7a, a written item: two dances from different places compared, with one reason their differences developed. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: many cultures and eras analyzed (grade 8). Tools: the journal page, venue card, research card, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed source pages. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in grade 7 because the family showing gathers the year's borrowed designs, studies and revisions, and a style's history needs unit 7's comparing of styles. Its family showing (weeks 30 and 31) is its own end-of-unit assessment, before the year-end check in week 32 | Candidate: the class list of styles checked for sources from each style's own community | Capoeira: the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's page and an Afro-Brazilian or Brazilian cultural body's page first; slavery is told in plain sentences, with no role play of enslaved people or enslavers. Capoeira's kicks and sweeps are not tried; the spirit study keeps to the sway, the circle and call and response, with no move aimed at a person. Weave: watched only; the agent reads Rosy Simas Danse's own pages and a Seneca Nation source first; she is named as a Seneca maker, the dance is hers, and no student imitates its movement, a ceremony or regalia. Tango is studied by watching; any partner frame is by agreement, 