98:   partner spacing drill); in grades 6 to 8 it is the five-minute warm-up and two or three fluency days a unit, more
99:   in units 1, 5 and 9. Application: making and rehearsing a dance with some choice, drafting and revising a critique
100:   or analysis; the second application day holds the unit's one critique (describe, ask, suggest), and the next day's
101:   rehearsal of the change is application too. Check: a showing scored live on the guide, or the written and show
102:   items of the unit's evidence line. Respond: a second try at the part marked "not yet", or a re-rehearsal before a
103:   second showing. A unit runs roughly: understanding, fluency, application, fluency, application with the critique,
104:   rehearsal, check, respond.
105: - **Scored evidence.** Every owned id has an end-of-unit item or a look-for, named id by id in the unit's
106:   "End-of-unit evidence" line. A progression row's "Assessed by" note names one form for the row, not one item for
107:   each id, so it is never the whole line. Items come in four kinds: a look-for on the showing's guide (three to five
108:   look-fors, met, partly or not yet), marked live by the teacher as each group dances; a show item, where the student
109:   shows a move at the teacher's spot inside the group's turn; a written item at tables on an unseen dance (a student
110:   may answer aloud or show it instead, and the mark is the same); and a kept card or written product (a log, a
111:   criteria sheet, a notation card, a research note) made on the unit's last making days and scored after the
112:   session. A written product scored in a unit (unit 11's analysis, each unit 3, 7 and 11 critique) has its draft
113:   day, the teacher's written feedback and its revision day counted in the unit before the score. Two kinds of check
114:   day, never mixed on one day. A showing day: set-up 3 minutes, warm-up 5, six groups of five dance in the marked
115:   zone for about 4 minutes each (24: about two minutes of dancing, then any show item) while the rest sit in the
116:   audience zone and practice audience manners (nothing else is asked of them), cool-down and the self-check on the
117:   guide 5, room back 3: 40 minutes. A written day: set-up 2, the teacher dances the unseen Curio Class phrase twice
118:   or hands out its written description (about 4), written items at tables (28), collecting 3: 37 minutes, with slack
119:   for a third showing of the phrase. Either way the teacher watches one group, never a room and a group at once; a
120:   group the teacher wants to see again may be recorded on the school's device that day (feet and bodies with no
121:   faces, or the whole body with the family's yes) and the recording stays there (APPROACH "Kept work"). Every unit
122:   has two check days, one of each kind. No score comes from the day a move, an element or a notation is first
123:   taught. Each unit opens with a baseline look at its main look-for during the first practice days (APPROACH
124:   "Growth"), so every student has two marks a unit and the growth is the change. One piece a unit goes into the
125:   portfolio folder (a card, a log page, a notation, a critique, or a recording kept on the school's device) with a
126:   one-line written reflection (bar criterion 8).
127: - **Taking part without dancing alone.** No student dances alone for the class: every showing is in a group of
128:   five, and a student may dance beside a partner or show at the teacher's spot (APPROACH "Supports"). A student who
129:   cannot move freely dances a seated or one-limb version, or takes the rehearsal director's, the counter's or the
130:   staging lead's role, and is marked on the same look-fors in that version. A student who does not want to be
131:   recorded is marked live.
