317: | 1 | 6 | Together in space and time. *How do dancers share space, hit the beat in threes and fours, and use muscle tension to make movement say something?* | 18 | da-g6-elements; da-g6-technique | DA:Pr4.1.6a; DA:Pr4.1.6b; DA:Pr4.1.6c; DA:Pr5.1.6a; DA:Pr5.1.6b; DA:Pr5.1.6c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding through the dances studied (review); creating through the partner and group sections the class builds. Works: stepping (United States, grown through the 1900s in the historically Black fraternities and sororities of colleges and universities, danced today), stamps, claps and slaps with accents in four, a piece by a named step team the unit plan picks, described and linked to a museum's or the team's own page; Johann Strauss II, The Blue Danube (Austria, 1867, public domain music), danced by the class as a waltz in three; a butoh work by Sankai Juku the unit plan picks (Japan, company founded in 1975 by Ushio Amagatsu; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), slow movement driven by muscle tension, described and linked to the company's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 5 floor and air paths with tight and flowing contrast (da-g5-elements) and a run of phrases with safe habits (da-g5-technique), each with its ten-minute reteach. DA:Pr4.1.6a: build partner and group sections by judging distance, varying paths and holding the eyes' focus near or far as planned. DA:Pr4.1.6b: mix sudden and sustained timing and land accents on the right beats in three-beat (the waltz) and four-beat (stepping) measures, counted aloud. DA:Pr4.1.6c: start movement from muscle tension to show dynamics, and tell bound from free-flowing movement and use each (butoh's held tension beside the waltz's flow). DA:Pr5.1.6a: direction changes, small jumps with soft landings, and clean transitions between phrases. DA:Pr5.1.6b: a safe warm-up built from basic anatomy (the big muscle groups and joints each move wakes), body signals (breath, heat, a pulled or sharp feeling means stop), space awareness, and food and water choices before a rehearsal for an invented dancer. DA:Pr5.1.6c: rehearse an ensemble section, spot what works, test fixes for a problem and track the group's growth on the rehearsal log. Words: formation, focus, measure, accent, bound, transition. Showing: group sections for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Pr4.1.6a, a guide look-for on the showing: partner and group spacing kept through a path change, eyes' focus near or far as planned; DA:Pr4.1.6b, a guide look-for on the showing: accents on the right beats in the three-beat and the four-beat section, with one sudden and one sustained change; DA:Pr4.1.6c, a show item at the teacher's spot: one short phrase danced bound then free-flowing, started from tension, each named; DA:Pr5.1.6a, a guide look-for on the showing: a direction change, a small jump landed softly and a clean transition; DA:Pr5.1.6b, a written item on the written day: a warm-up plan card for an invented dancer (three moves and the body part each wakes, one body signal to stop for, one space check, one food or water choice before rehearsal); DA:Pr5.1.6c, the kept rehearsal log from the last rehearsal days (what worked, one problem, the fix tested, the change since the baseline), scored after the session. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: separate tempos in separate body parts (grade 8), syncopation (Level 1). Tools: the elements card, the counts card for three and four, a warm-up step card, the rehearsal log, work cards, all Curio Class's own; a beat keeper or the printed counts card. Free path: a drum or a pot, claps, floor tape or chalk. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First because every later unit needs the floor, the warm-up, the stop cue, partner spacing and the rehearsal log (the dance-warm-up, phrase-recall and rehearsal-goals models return here first, where they are first used this year). Longest unit of the year: six ids, three technique steps (jumps and landings, the anatomy warm-up, ensemble fixes) that need fluency days, and the year's routines set up. Physical education's dance sequences (its unit 6, grade 6 weeks 22 to 27) come later in the year, so this unit uses moves students already have | Candidate: a waltz or another three-beat social dance from outside Europe beside Strauss, for range | Hazards and controls: jumps small and landed through the feet with soft knees, on wood or mats, never on concrete or tile; step stamps in soft shoes or bare feet, slaps on the thighs and arms light; partner work keeps arm's length unless both agree to contact; no lifts; floor checked first; water and rest offered. Stepping: it grew in Black fraternities and sororities and carries their history and pride; the sensitivity agent reads a museum's page and a step team's or organization's own published words first, the class dances step patterns the unit plan writes, and no student copies an organization's own calls, chants or signs. Butoh: some works carry postwar themes; the unit plan picks a piece without them, and no student wears body paint. The invented dancer carries every food and water choice; no student is asked about their own eating or body. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
318: | 2 | 6 | Ideas that agree and ideas that clash. *How can two ideas that agree or clash, tried in different styles, become a dance study with a goal?* | 14 | da-g6-ideas; da-g6-form | DA:Cr1.1.6a; DA:Cr1.1.6b; DA:Cr2.1.6a; DA:Cr2.1.6b | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: Lucinda Childs, Dance (United States, 1979; Childs born 1940, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), made with Philip Glass's music and Sol LeWitt's film of the dancers, three arts joined, described and linked to the company's page; Jerome Robbins, Fancy Free (United States, 1944; Robbins died 1998), ballet mixed with the social dances of its day, its three sailors' solos only, described and linked to New York City Ballet's or a dance archive's page: one maker joins ideas from other arts, the other joins styles | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 5 material from several sources (da-g5-ideas) and several tools altered together (da-g5-form), each with its reteach. DA:Cr1.1.6a: join ideas from two different inspirations (a picture and a sound, a poem line and a place, a nature process and a chance roll) by what they share or how they clash, on the inspiration list. DA:Cr1.1.6b: try one idea in three styles from the style menu (ballet, modern, jazz, hip-hop, a social dance the class knows) and see how each changes the steps. DA:Cr2.1.6a: build a study of about 32 counts with choreographic tools (repetition, canon, retrograde, accumulation) and a form (AB, ABA or rondo), its goal stated in one line before it is danced. DA:Cr2.1.6b: write three criteria for a personal or cultural study (an invented dancer's or a public figure's option marked the same) and judge which of two versions of a move says it best. Words: inspiration, contrast, style, canon, form, criterion. Showing: the studies shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Cr1.1.6a, the kept inspiration list: two ideas, the joining word (alike or opposite) and the moves taken from each; DA:Cr1.1.6b, a written item on the written day: the styles note, three styles tried on one idea and one feature of each that changed the steps; DA:Cr2.1.6a, a guide look-for on the showing: at least two tools and the form seen, and the goal stated before dancing; DA:Cr2.1.6b, the kept criteria sheet: three criteria and the judged pair of moves with a reason from the criteria. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: sources compared and styles chosen for an aim (grade 7). Tools: the inspiration list, style menu, tools card and criteria sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: picture and sound cards printed, a die for the chance roll. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 1 because a study needs the spacing, accents and tension just taught as its material; before unit 3 because unit 3 revises this study against group criteria. The style-menu model starts here, where it is first needed | Candidate: a study inspiration from a maker outside the United States beside Childs and Robbins | Personal studies are invited, never required: the invented dancer's or public figure's option is offered beside them and marked the same; a cultural study uses a public tradition from a source, never a classmate's family. Fancy Free's sailors flirt and scuffle; the unit uses the three solos only. Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: canon and traveling in groups keep the marked lanes; the stop cue; floor checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
319: | 3 | 6 | Criteria and critique. *What makes a dance work, judged by criteria we write ourselves, and how can we write down the moves we keep?* | 14 | da-g6-revise; da-g6-respond | DA:Cr3.1.6a; DA:Cr3.1.6b; DA:Re7.1.6a; DA:Re7.1.6b; DA:Re8.1.6a; DA:Re9.1.6a | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9). Works: Rennie Harris, an excerpt of Rome & Jewels (United States, 2000; Rennie Harris Puremovement, Philadelphia; born 1964, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), hip-hop dance theater from Romeo and Juliet, described and linked to the company's page; stepping from unit 1, revisited beside it: how time and energy work in two styles; the class's own studies from unit 2 | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 5 change log (da-g5-revise) and meaning from movement patterns (da-g5-respond), each with its reteach. DA:Cr3.1.6a: revise the unit 2 study against criteria the group builds, and explain how each revision fits the intent. DA:Cr3.1.6b: invent a way to write down a sequence of moves (stick figures, symbols, a count grid) and test it by having a partner dance from it. DA:Re7.1.6a: describe or show the repeating movement patterns in a dance and how they relate (one answers another, one grows from another). DA:Re7.1.6b: explain how one element (time, space or energy) works in two styles, hip-hop and stepping, with specific dance terms. DA:Re8.1.6a: explain how elements, body use, technique, structure and setting carry a dance's purpose. DA:Re9.1.6a: discuss a dance's features and intent, then draft criteria and write a critique with them (the criteria card begins), drafted, given written feedback and revised inside the unit. Class discussion practices SL.6.1, not owned. Words: pattern, relationship, style, purpose, critique, notation. Showing: the revised studies shown to a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Cr3.1.6a, a guide look-for on the showing of the revised study, with the kept revision note (each revision and how it fits the intent); DA:Cr3.1.6b, the kept sequence written in the student's own method, scored on whether a partner could dance it; DA:Re7.1.6a, a written item on the unseen teacher-danced phrase: its repeating patterns and how they relate (shown at the teacher's spot instead if the student chooses); DA:Re7.1.6b, a written item: one element compared in the unseen phrase and one studied style, with two dance terms; DA:Re8.1.6a, a written item on the unseen dance's written description (its setting given): how two of elements, technique, structure and setting carry its purpose; DA:Re9.1.6a, the revised critique of Rome & Jewels with the student's own three criteria, scored after its revision day. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an established notation (grade 7). Tools: the criteria card, the notation sketch card, the critique frame, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: paper and pencil for notation. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 2 because it revises unit 2's study; the critique needs unit 1's elements words and unit 2's criteria. The dance-criteria-card model starts here, where it is first needed | Candidate: a third style for the element comparison from Asia or Latin America | Rome & Jewels tells a story of rival crews and a death; the unit plan picks an excerpt without the fight scenes, and the sensitivity agent reads the company's page and Harris's own published words first; hip-hop is taught as an art with its own history in Philadelphia and New York. Critique: feedback names a criterion, never a classmate's body or looks. Hazards and controls: hip-hop moves only from the floor up, no spins on the head or hands; floor checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
320: | 4 | 6 | Dance with a point of view. *How can a dance take a stand on an issue, and what does a dance's movement mean to the people who made it?* | 14 | da-g6-stage; da-g6-connect | DA:Pr6.1.6a; DA:Pr6.1.6b; DA:Cn10.1.6a; DA:Cn10.1.6b; DA:Cn11.1.6a | Performing (Pr6); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Kurt Jooss, The Green Table (Germany, 1932; Jooss died 1979), a dance against war set around a table of talking diplomats, described and linked to a ballet company's or a dance archive's page; figurines of dancers from Tang dynasty tombs (China, 7th to 8th century, a museum's open-access collection; the unit plan picks the figurine and records its page), with the whirling dance that reached China along the Silk Roads from Sogdiana in Central Asia, studied by looking only; Santee Smith, Kaha:wi (Kanien'keh�:ka, Six Nations of the Grand River, Ontario, Canada, 2004; Kaha:wi Dance Theatre; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a dance of family and generations, described and linked to the company's page, watched only | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 5 one dance adapted to two spaces (da-g5-stage) and a dance from another subject's topic (da-g5-connect), each with its reteach. DA:Pr6.1.6a: adapt the moves to the performing space, practice performance manners (entering, holding stillness, the bow, being an audience) and apply the choreographer's notes. DA:Pr6.1.6b: compare production elements (the room's lights on or off or daylight, plain clothing colors, the class's own sound, a light prop), choose some and explain the choices in production terms. DA:Cn10.1.6a: compare the qualities of one style studied with the student's own movement tastes in the log, or with an invented dancer's, marked the same. DA:Cn10.1.6b: research a social issue from the class list (shared spaces and litter, water, being left out, noise and quiet) on the research card, make a group dance that takes a viewpoint on it, and discuss how the research changed the student's thinking. DA:Cn11.1.6a: show and interpret how a dance's movement carries a cultural, historical or community meaning (the Tang figurines' whirl and what it says about trade and taste; Kaha:wi's generations, in the company's own words). The Silk Roads trade comes from social studies grade 6 (Cross-subject). Words: etiquette, note, production, viewpoint, research, meaning. Showing: the viewpoint dances for families, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Pr6.1.6a, a guide look-for on the family showing: moves fit the space, manners kept, one choreographer's note applied; DA:Pr6.1.6b, the kept production card: two elements compared, one chosen, the reason in two production terms; DA:Cn10.1.6a, a written item on the written day: one quality of a studied style compared with the student's or the invented dancer's tastes; DA:Cn10.1.6b, a guide look-for on the showing (the viewpoint seen in the dance), with the kept research card's "what changed my thinking" line; DA:Cn11.1.6a, a written item on a work card (a Tang figurine or Kaha:wi): one movement and the meaning it carries for its people, time or place, shown at the teacher's spot instead if the student chooses. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a style's history researched (grade 7). Tools: the manners card, production card, research card, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: the room's own light and the class's sound. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in the year because the family showing gathers the year's spacing, studies and revisions (units 1 to 3), and social studies' Silk Roads unit comes before it. The performance-etiquette model starts here. Its family showing (weeks 30 and 31) is its own end-of-unit assessment, before the year-end check in week 32 | Candidate: the issue list checked for party politics and for any issue that asks a student to tell their own hardship | The Green Table: its figure of Death and its soldiers are studied by watching and talk; no student plays a victim, a soldier or Death (no role play of harm). Tang figurines are tomb objects: named as burial objects and studied by looking only. Kaha:wi: the sensitivity agent reads Kaha:wi Dance Theatre's own pages and a Six Nations or Haudenosaunee source first; the unit names Kanien'keh�:ka (Mohawk), says the dance is the company's, and no student imitates its movement or dress. Social issues come from the class list; a student is never asked to tell their own hardship, and an invented or public option is offered. Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: group traveling in lanes; light props only; stage edge marked; floor checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
321: | 5 | 7 | Borrowed shapes and growing bodies. *What can dancers borrow from other styles' shapes and accents, and how do they keep their technique safe as bodies change?* | 18 | da-g7-elements; da-g7-technique | DA:Pr4.1.7a; DA:Pr4.1.7b; DA:Pr4.1.7c; DA:Pr5.1.7a; DA:Pr5.1.7b; DA:Pr5.1.7c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding through the dances studied (review); creating through the phrases the class builds. Works: the Nicholas Brothers, the Jumpin' Jive number in the film Stormy Weather (United States, 1943; Fayard died 2006, Harold 2000), tap and jazz with sharp accents and wide aerial designs, described and linked, never copied; a rehearsal of Dance Theatre of Harlem (United States, founded 1969 by Arthur Mitchell and Karel Shook), shown on the company's own pages, professionals setting rehearsal goals; Sankai Juku's butoh from unit 1, revisited beside them: three styles' qualities compared | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 6 partner spacing, accents and tension (da-g7 assumes da-g6-elements, unit 1) and clean transitions and the anatomy warm-up (da-g6-technique, unit 1). DA:Pr4.1.7a: widen floor and air patterns by borrowing body designs (symmetrical and asymmetrical shapes) from tap and jazz, ballet and butoh. DA:Pr4.1.7b: vary timing within phrases with accents and variations for interest (syncopation is Level 1's). DA:Pr4.1.7c: compare movement qualities across two styles, describe them in vivid words, and plan how to perform them clearly. DA:Pr5.1.7a: adapt technique as dancers' bodies grow, applying alignment and weight shifts (balance and landings practiced, never forced), taught about dancers in general. DA:Pr5.1.7b: practice healthy habits and good nutrition in dance and daily life through an invented dancer, and discuss the benefits. DA:Pr5.1.7c: rehearse with peers and set group rehearsal goals after watching the professionals' rehearsal. Words: body design, asymmetry, accent, variation, alignment, weight shift. Showing: borrowed-design phrases for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Pr4.1.7a, a guide look-for on the showing: one symmetrical and one asymmetrical borrowed design in the floor and air pattern, its style named; DA:Pr4.1.7b, a guide look-for on the showing: an accented variation that changes the phrase's timing; DA:Pr4.1.7c, a written item on the written day: two styles' qualities compared in vivid words and one line of the performing plan; DA:Pr5.1.7a, a show item at the teacher's spot: a weight shift and an aligned landing, with one adaptation the student chose said aloud (about any dancer's balance, never the student's own body); DA:Pr5.1.7b, a written item: the invented dancer's habit card (rest, water, food before rehearsal, sleep), with one benefit each; DA:Pr5.1.7c, the kept group goals note written after the professionals' rehearsal, with the group's rehearsal against it, scored after the session. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: separate tempos in separate body parts (grade 8). Tools: the accent card, the style menu's body designs, the habit card, the goals note, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: claps and counts for accents. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 7 because it reopens the floor, the warm-up and the rehearsal routine after the summer and moves the elements from spacing to borrowing; the accent-and-timing model starts here, where it is first needed. Physical education's grade 7 dance unit (its unit 13, weeks 22 to 26) comes later in the year | Candidate: a fourth style's body designs from Africa or South Asia, for range | Growing bodies: taught about dancers in general ("balance and landings can feel different during a growth spurt"); no student is asked about their own body, height, weight or growth, and the adaptation is the student's own choice. The invented dancer carries every food and habit item. The Nicholas Brothers worked in a segregated film industry; the unit says so in one plain sentence from a film archive's or museum's page, read first by the sensitivity agent. No student tries the brothers' splits, flips or stair jumps. Hazards and controls: landings through the feet with soft knees on wood or mats; no stretch pushed by a partner; no lifts; water and rest. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
322: | 6 | 7 | Choosing what inspires. *Which sources and styles best serve what a dance means to say, and how do our criteria show it?* | 14 | da-g7-ideas; da-g7-form | DA:Cr1.1.7a; DA:Cr1.1.7b; DA:Cr2.1.7a; DA:Cr2.1.7b | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: Shen Wei, Rite of Spring (United States, 2003, Shen Wei Dance Arts; Shen born 1968 in China, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Stravinsky's score danced as moving brushwork across a painted floor grid, described and linked to the company's page; Twyla Tharp, Deuce Coupe (United States, 1973; born 1941, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), ballet mixed with the social dances of the 1960s, described and linked: two makers who chose a source and a style on purpose | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 6 ideas joined by likeness or clash and the style menu (da-g6-ideas, unit 2) and a study with a goal and criteria (da-g6-form, unit 2). DA:Cr1.1.7a: compare several sources of inspiration (a brush stroke, a song, a place, a news photo from the class set) and use the chosen ones to widen the student's moves and expressive range. DA:Cr1.1.7b: choose movement styles that serve the dance's aim and explain the choices in dance terms. DA:Cr2.1.7a: shape a study with several tools and forms (theme and variation, call and response, canon) and explain the choices. DA:Cr2.1.7b: define criteria for a personal or cultural study (an invented dancer's or a public figure's option marked the same) and explain how they carry its meaning. Words: source, range, aim, variation, intent, criteria. Showing: the studies shown to another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Cr1.1.7a, the kept source comparison card: three sources compared, two chosen, and the new moves each added; DA:Cr1.1.7b, a written item on the written day: the styles chosen and why each serves the aim, in two dance terms; DA:Cr2.1.7a, a guide look-for on the showing, with a spoken or written line explaining one tool and the form; DA:Cr2.1.7b, the kept criteria sheet with each criterion's link to the study's meaning explained. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an original dance made with a group (grade 8). Tools: the source card, the style menu, the tools card, the criteria sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: printed source cards. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 5 because the study draws on the borrowed designs and accents just taught; before unit 7, which weighs revisions to this study | Candidate: a source from a Native or African maker on the source cards, for range | Personal and cultural studies have an invented or public option marked the same; a cultural study uses a public tradition from its own source. Shen Wei draws on Chinese painting and opera, studied with the company's own words; no student imitates a culture. News photos come from the class set the unit plan chooses, with no injury or disaster. Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: canon in lanes; floor checked; the stop cue. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
323: | 7 | 7 | Weighing changes, comparing meanings. *How do dancers judge which change makes a dance clearer, and why can two dances mean such different things?* | 14 | da-g7-revise; da-g7-respond | DA:Cr3.1.7a; DA:Cr3.1.7b; DA:Re7.1.7a; DA:Re7.1.7b; DA:Re8.1.7a; DA:Re9.1.7a | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9). Works: Labanotation (Rudolf Laban, published 1928) and its simpler motif notation, studied from the Dance Notation Bureau's own pages (New York, founded 1940), an established method; Mark Morris, L'Allegro, il Penseroso ed il Moderato (United States, 1988, Mark Morris Dance Group; Morris born 1956, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), modern dance on Handel's music, an excerpt the unit plan picks, described and linked to the company's page; a stage folk piece by the Moiseyev Dance Company the unit plan picks (Moscow, founded 1937 by Igor Moiseyev, 1906 to 2007), folk dances of many peoples arranged for the stage, described and linked to the company's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 6 group criteria and an own notation (da-g6-revise, unit 3) and the criteria card critique (da-g6-respond, unit 3). DA:Cr3.1.7a: weigh possible revisions to the unit 6 study, adjusting the student's own standards when needed, and explain how the kept ones make the intent clearer. DA:Cr3.1.7b: study an established notation method (motif symbols for direction, level, body part and timing) and record a sequence with it in writing and signs, or a recording on the school's device. DA:Re7.1.7a: compare movement patterns in two dances and how they link. DA:Re7.1.7b: contrast how elements are used in Morris's modern dance and Moiseyev's stage folk dance, with specialized terms. DA:Re8.1.7a: compare what two dances mean and how elements, technique and setting shape each meaning. DA:Re9.1.7a: compare two dances' purposes, subjects and settings to study their styles, then refine the criteria card, with a written comparison drafted, given written feedback and revised inside the unit. Words: revision, standard, notation, motif, contrast, setting. Showing: the revised studies for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: DA:Cr3.1.7a, a guide look-for on the showing of the revised study, with the kept change log (revisions weighed, the standard adjusted, how each kept change clarifies intent); DA:Cr3.1.7b, the kept notation card of an eight-count sequence, scored on whether a partner can read it back; DA:Re7.1.7a, a written item on the unseen teacher-danced phrase beside one studied dance: their patterns and links compared; DA:Re7.1.7b, a written item: one element contrasted across the two styles in two specialized terms; DA:Re8.1.7a, a written item on two short written descriptions: what each dance means and one element or setting that shapes each; DA:Re9.1.7a, the revised written comparison of L'Allegro and the Moiseyev piece with the refined criteria card, scored after its revision day. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an excerpt notated from a longer dance (grade 8). Tools: the notation card, the change log, the comparison frame, work cards, Curio Class's own; a notation practice tool or its printed card. Free path: printed symbol sheets. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6 because it revises unit 6's study; comparing meanings needs unit 5's quality words. The dance-notation model starts here, where it is first needed | Candidate: the Dance Notation Bureau's pages checked open and free on the day the unit plan is written | The Moiseyev company arranged village dances of many peoples for the stage; the unit says the stage versions differ from the village ones, the sensitivity agent reads the company's own page first, and no student imitates a people's dress or accent. Recordings for notation are optional, of feet and bodies with no faces unless the family says yes, and stay on the school's device. Hazards and controls: the Moiseyev piece's deep squats, leaps and fast spins not tried (turns kept to one slow turn in place); floor checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
324: | 8 | 7 | Where styles come from. *What does a dance style's history tell us about its spirit, and why do dances from different places differ?* | 14 | da-g7-stage; da-g7-connect | DA:Pr6.1.7a; DA:Pr6.1.7b; DA:Cn10.1.7a; DA:Cn10.1.7b; DA:Cn11.1.7a | Performing (Pr6); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: capoeira (Brazil, made by enslaved Africans and their descendants; on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage since 2014 as the capoeira circle), described and linked to UNESCO's page; Argentine tango (Buenos Aires and Montevideo, late 1800s; on UNESCO's list since 2009), described and linked to UNESCO's page; Rosy Simas, Weave (Seneca Nation; Rosy Simas Danse, Minneapolis, 2018; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a dance made with Native and other dancers about belonging, described and linked to the company's page, watched only | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 6 manners, notes and production terms (da-g6-stage, unit 4) and the viewpoint dance and cultural meaning (da-g6-connect, unit 4). DA:Pr6.1.7a: recommend adaptations to the performing space, follow etiquette, keep a rehearsal journal and apply the choreographer's notes. DA:Pr6.1.7b: explore producing the dance for another venue or audience (the gym, outdoors, a younger class) and explain how production elements would change. DA:Cn10.1.7a: contrast movement qualities across two styles with the student's own, or with an invented dancer's, marked the same, and discuss how each shows a perspective. DA:Cn10.1.7b: research a style's history from the class list (capoeira, tango, tap, ballet, hip-hop) on the research card, and present a study of its spirit made of the student's own moves, never an imitation of the style. DA:Cn11.1.7a: compare dances from different places (capoeira and tango, or tango and Weave) and suggest why the differences developed. Words: venue, journal, history, spirit, perspective, tradition. Showing: the spirit studies fo