ow it fits the intent); DA:Cr3.1.6b, the kept sequence written in the student's own method, scored on whether a partner could dance it; DA:Re7.1.6a, a written item on the unseen teacher-danced phrase: its repeating patterns and how they relate (shown at the teacher's spot instead if the student chooses); DA:Re7.1.6b, a written item: one element compared in the unseen phrase and one studied style, with two dance terms; DA:Re8.1.6a, a written item on the unseen dance's written description (its setting given): how two of elements, technique, structure and setting carry its purpose; DA:Re9.1.6a, the revised critique of Rome & Jewels with the student's own three criteria, scored after its revision day. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an established notation (grade 7). Tools: the criteria card, the notation sketch card, the critique frame, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: paper and pencil for notation. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 2 because it revises unit 2's study; the critique needs unit 1's elements words and unit 2's criteria. The dance-criteria-card model starts here, where it is first needed | Candidate: a third style for the element comparison from Asia or Latin America | Rome & Jewels tells a story of rival crews and a death; the unit plan picks an excerpt without the fight scenes, and the sensitivity agent reads the company's page and Harris's own published words first; hip-hop is taught as an art with its own history in Philadelphia and New York. Critique: feedback names a criterion, never a classmate's body or looks. Hazards and controls: hip-hop moves only from the floor up, no spins on the head or hands; floor checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
