"da","6","da-g6-ideas","DA:Cr1.1.6a; DA:Cr1.1.6b","da-g5-ideas","da-g7-ideas","inspiration-list; movement-challenge","inspiration-list; movement-challenge; style-menu","alike; opposite; style; connect; choreography; express","","Join ideas from different inspirations by what they share or how they clash to build a short dance, and try movement styles to turn those ideas into steps. What changes: ideas are joined by likeness or contrast, and styles are tried on purpose, where grade 5 gathered material from several sources. Assessed by a short dance idea showing and a written note on the styles tried. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"da","7","da-g7-ideas","DA:Cr1.1.7a; DA:Cr1.1.7b","da-g6-ideas","da-g8-ideas","inspiration-list; movement-challenge; style-menu","inspiration-list; movement-challenge; style-menu","compare; broaden; range; expressive; aim; dance terms","","Compare several sources of inspiration and use the chosen ones to widen the student's moves and range, then pick movement styles that fit the aim and explain them with dance terms. What changes: choices are compared and defended with dance words. Assessed by a showing plus a short written or spoken reason using dance terms, scored on a short rubric."
"da","8","da-g8-ideas","DA:Cr1.1.8a; DA:Cr1.1.8b","da-g7-ideas","da-g9-ideas","inspiration-list; movement-challenge; style-menu","inspiration-list; movement-challenge; style-menu","study; inspiration; favorite; original; justify; style terms","","Turn several sources into movement material for a dance study, then choose the student's own strongest moves for an original dance and justify them with style terms. What changes: the dancer owns the material and defends the picks. Assessed by a portfolio entry of the study and a justification, scored on a short rubric."
"da","6","da-g6-form","DA:Cr2.1.6a; DA:Cr2.1.6b","da-g5-form","da-g7-form","start-middle-finish; choreographic-tools","start-middle-finish; choreographic-tools","purposeful; goal; criteria; personal; cultural; judge","","Build a dance study toward a goal you set, using choreographic tools and forms, and write criteria for a personal or cultural study to judge which moves say it best. What changes: the study has a stated goal and the student's own criteria, where grade 5 built a study around a main idea. Assessed by a performed study plus a criteria sheet scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"da","7","da-g7-form","DA:Cr2.1.7a; DA:Cr2.1.7b","da-g6-form","da-g8-form","start-middle-finish; choreographic-tools","start-middle-finish; choreographic-tools","shape; vary; criteria; meaning; explain; study","","Shape a dance study with several tools and forms and explain the choices, and write criteria for a personal or cultural study that say how it carries its meaning. What changes: criteria are defined and explained, where grade 6 wrote and applied them. Assessed by a performed study and a written explanation scored on a short rubric."
"da","8","da-g8-form","DA:Cr2.1.8a; DA:Cr2.1.8b","da-g7-form","da-g9-form","start-middle-finish; choreographic-tools","start-middle-finish; choreographic-tools","together; new dance; group; process; apply; effect","","With a group, choose tools and a form for a new dance and keep a note on how the group worked, then write criteria for a personal or cultural dance and use them on the piece. What changes: the work is made with others and the group process is described, where grade 7 made a study alone. Assessed by a group performance and a process description, scored on a short rubric."
"da","6","da-g6-revise","DA:Cr3.1.6a; DA:Cr3.1.6b","da-g5-revise","da-g7-revise","try-a-change; feedback-loop; change-log","try-a-change; feedback-loop; change-log","criteria; group; revise; fit; own method; write down","","Revise a dance against criteria your group built and explain how each revision fits the intent, and invent your own way of writing down a sequence of moves. What changes: the group builds the criteria and the student invents a way to record moves, where grade 5 logged changes. Assessed by a revised dance and a written sequence in the student's own method, scored on a short rubric."
"da","7","da-g7-revise","DA:Cr3.1.7a; DA:Cr3.1.7b","da-g6-revise","da-g8-revise","try-a-change; feedback-loop; change-log","try-a-change; feedback-loop; change-log; dance-notation","judge; adjust; standards; notation; established; record","","Weigh possible revisions, adjusting the student's own standards when needed, and explain how they make the intent clearer, then try an established notation method to record a sequence in writing, signs or a recording. What changes: revisions are judged against shifting standards and a real notation method is studied. Assessed by a revised dance and a notated sequence, scored on a short rubric."
"da","8","da-g8-revise","DA:Cr3.1.8a; DA:Cr3.1.8b","da-g7-revise","da-g9-revise","try-a-change; feedback-loop; change-log; dance-notation","try-a-change; feedback-loop; change-log; dance-notation","alone; collaborative; excerpt; strengthen; part; notation","","Revise choreography alone or with others against criteria and feedback and explain how the changes strengthen the intent, and record a dance excerpt with parts of an established notation method. What changes: the revision is independent or shared and an excerpt is notated. Assessed by a revised piece and a notated excerpt, scored on a short rubric."
"da","6","da-g6-elements","DA:Pr4.1.6a; DA:Pr4.1.6b; DA:Pr4.1.6c","da-g5-elements","da-g7-elements","dance-elements; energy-words","dance-elements; energy-words","partner; group; abrupt; lingering; measure; tension","","Build partner and group sections by judging distance and varying paths, mix sudden and sustained timing on the right beats in three- and four-beat music, use muscle tension to start movement and show dynamics, use the eyes' focus near or far, and tell bound from free-flowing movement and use each. What changes: partner and group spacing and measures of three and four. Assessed by a group performance scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: no lifts; partner contact only by agreement and never required; a solo version is always offered."
"da","7","da-g7-elements","DA:Pr4.1.7a; DA:Pr4.1.7b; DA:Pr4.1.7c","da-g6-elements","da-g8-elements","dance-elements; energy-words","dance-elements; energy-words; accent-and-timing","borrow; style; accent; variation; compare; plan","","Widen floor and air patterns by borrowing body designs from other styles, vary timing with accents, and compare movement qualities across styles before planning how to perform them clearly. What changes: styles are compared on their elements, where grade 6 refined partner spacing. Assessed by a performed phrase and a short comparison, scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: a style is borrowed only from a community's own published teaching or a named practitioner. A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it. no lifts; partner contact only by agreement and never required; a solo version is always offered."
"da","8","da-g8-elements","DA:Pr4.1.8a; DA:Pr4.1.8b; DA:Pr4.1.8c","da-g7-elements","da-g9-elements","dance-elements; energy-words; accent-and-timing","dance-elements; energy-words; accent-and-timing","sculpt; props; surroundings; breath; separate tempo; texture","","Shape the body around other dancers, props and the room while holding focus, choose beat, movement and breath phrasing with different tempos in different body parts, and texture movement by steering energy. What changes: patterns are complex and timing is layered, where grade 7 varied accents. Assessed by a performed phrase scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: no lifts; partner contact only by agreement and never required; a solo version is always offered."
"da","6","da-g6-technique","DA:Pr5.1.6a; DA:Pr5.1.6b; DA:Pr5.1.6c","da-g5-technique","da-g7-technique","dance-warm-up; phrase-recall; rehearsal-goals","dance-warm-up; phrase-recall; rehearsal-goals","direction change; jump; landing; transition; anatomy; warm up","physical-education:g6-dance-sequences (eight-count phrases and transitions)","Carry out direction changes, jumps, landings and transitions cleanly, warm up safely using anatomy, body signals, space awareness and food and water choices (through an invented dancer), and track growth, and improve an ensemble dance by spotting what works and testing fixes. What changes: transitions are clean and the warm-up is understood, where grade 5 set rehearsal goals. Assessed by a performed ensemble dance, a warm-up plan card and a growth note, scored on a short rubric. Physical education teaches the motor phrases. Teacher note: no lifts; partner contact only by agreement and never required; a solo version is always offered. Teacher note: no task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body; food choices and eating plans are studied through an invented dancer or dancers in general (APPROACH.md, ""Sensitive ground"")."
"da","7","da-g7-technique","DA:Pr5.1.7a; DA:Pr5.1.7b; DA:Pr5.1.7c","da-g6-technique","da-g8-technique","dance-warm-up; phrase-recall; rehearsal-goals","dance-warm-up; phrase-recall; rehearsal-goals","adapt; growing; weight shift; healthy; peers; group goals","physical-education:g7-dance-ideas (movement ideas in dance forms)","Adjust technique to a growing body while applying alignment and weight shifts, practice healthy habits and discuss the benefits, and set group rehearsal goals after watching professional dancers. What changes: technique is adapted to the changing body. Assessed by a rehearsed ensemble piece, the invented dancer's habit card and a goals note, scored on a short rubric. Physical education teaches the dance ideas. Teacher note: no lifts; partner contact only by agreement and never required; a solo version is always offered. Teacher note: no task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body; food choices and eating plans are studied through an invented dancer or dancers in general (APPROACH.md, ""Sensitive ground"")."
"da","8","da-g8-technique","DA:Pr5.1.8a; DA:Pr5.1.8b; DA:Pr5.1.8c","da-g7-technique","da-g9-technique","dance-warm-up; phrase-recall; rehearsal-goals","dance-warm-up; phrase-recall; rehearsal-goals","embody; spatial design; rhythmic phrase; evaluate; accuracy; record","physical-education:g8-fitness-dance-apply (dance routines done with correct form); health:g8-phw-habits-personal-community (healthy habits in dance and daily life)","Copy, recall and perform spatial designs and rhythmic phrases, rate health habits in dance and daily life, using an invented dancer for food and body, and plan ways to improve, and work with peers on accuracy while tracking your own progress. What changes: personal goals and a progress record are kept, where grade 7 set group goals. Assessed by a performed piece, a habit rating card and a progress record, scored on a short rubric. Physical education teaches the routine form. Teacher note: no lifts; partner contact only by agreement and never required; a solo version is always offered. Teacher note: no task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body; food choices and eating plans are studied through an invented dancer or dancers in general (APPROACH.md, ""Sensitive ground"")."
"da","6","da-g6-stage","DA:Pr6.1.6a; DA:Pr6.1.6b","da-g5-stage","da-g7-stage","performing-space; stage-terms","performing-space; stage-terms; performance-etiquette","manners; notes; choreographer; production terms; compare; choose","","Adapt moves to the performance space, practice performance manners, apply the choreographer's notes, and compare production elements, explaining the choices with production terms. What changes: etiquette and notes are applied and choices are explained in production terms, where grade 5 adapted one dance to venues. Assessed by a performed dance and an explanation, scored on a short rubric."
"da","7","da-g7-stage","DA:Pr6.1.7a; DA:Pr6.1.7b","da-g6-stage","da-g8-stage","performing-space; stage-terms; performance-etiquette","performing-space; stage-terms; performance-etiquette","recommend; etiquette; journal; venue; audience; explain","","Recommend how to adapt a space, follow etiquette, keep a rehearsal journal and apply the choreographer's notes, and explain how production elements would change for another venue or audience. What changes: a rehearsal journal and recommendations for other venues, where grade 6 explained choices once. Assessed by a performed dance and its journal, scored on a short rubric."
"da","8","da-g8-stage","DA:Pr6.1.8a; DA:Pr6.1.8b","da-g7-stage","da-g9-stage","performing-space; stage-terms; performance-etiquette","performing-space; stage-terms; performance-etiquette","lead; prepare; document; improve; carry out; design","","Lead warm-up and preparation, follow stage etiquette, keep a rehearsal record with improvements and applied notes, and design and stage features with a group for more than one space. What changes: students lead and design, where grade 7 recommended and journaled. Assessed by a led showing, a preparation record and a staging design, scored on a short rubric."
"da","6","da-g6-respond","DA:Re7.1.6a; DA:Re7.1.6b; DA:Re8.1.6a; DA:Re9.1.6a","da-g5-respond","da-g7-respond","look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret; dance-reason-because","look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret; dance-reason-because; dance-criteria-card","relate; terms; elements; technique; criteria; critique","ela:SL.6.1 (discussion with evidence)","Write a critique of a dance with criteria the student drafts, using specific dance terms for how its patterns relate, how its elements compare with another style, and how technique, structure and setting carry its purpose. What changes: the student builds the criteria, where grade 5 defined what makes a dance artistic. Assessed by a written critique using their criteria, scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"da","7","da-g7-respond","DA:Re7.1.7a; DA:Re7.1.7b; DA:Re8.1.7a; DA:Re9.1.7a","da-g6-respond","da-g8-respond","look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret; dance-reason-because; dance-criteria-card","look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret; dance-reason-because; dance-criteria-card","weigh; contrast; specialized terms; settings; refine; purpose","","Compare movement patterns and how elements are used across styles with specialized terms, compare what two dances mean, and refine the student's criteria by comparing their purposes, subjects and settings. What changes: criteria are compared and refined, where grade 6 built them. Assessed by a written comparison scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"da","8","da-g8-respond","DA:Re7.1.8a; DA:Re7.1.8b; DA:Re8.1.8a; DA:Re9.1.8a","da-g7-respond","da-g9-respond","look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret; dance-reason-because; dance-criteria-card","look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret; dance-reason-because; dance-criteria-card","evidence; express; intent; judge; effective; context","ela:W.8.1 (an argument supported by evidence)","Watch a chosen dance several times and write a short analysis with time-stamped or moment-named evidence: the patterns and what they are for, how its elements show intent beside another style, and a judgment of the performance by criteria. What changes: evidence is cited for a judgment of an effective performance, where grade 7 compared and refined criteria. Assessed by a written response with evidence scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"da","6","da-g6-connect","DA:Cn10.1.6a; DA:Cn10.1.6b; DA:Cn11.1.6a","da-g5-connect","da-g7-connect","like-me-line; dance-two-ties; research-notes","like-me-line; dance-two-ties; research-notes","attitude; taste; social issue; viewpoint; history; meaning","social-studies:ss6-silk-roads-exchange (art motifs moving with trade)","Make a dance that takes a viewpoint on a researched social issue, compare a style's qualities with the student's own movement tastes in the log, and interpret what a dance's moves mean to its culture, time or community. What changes: a social issue is researched and a viewpoint is danced, where grade 5 compared learning across subjects. Assessed by a performed piece, a short reflection, a log comparison and a cultural-meaning item, scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"da","7","da-g7-connect","DA:Cn10.1.7a; DA:Cn10.1.7b; DA:Cn11.1.7a","da-g6-connect","da-g8-connect","like-me-line; dance-two-ties; research-notes","like-me-line; dance-two-ties; research-notes","perspective; contrast; history; spirit; present; communities","","Compare movement qualities across styles with the student's own and discuss how each shows a viewpoint, research a style's history and present a study of its spirit, and compare dances from different places with reasons they differ. What changes: a style's history and the reasons for differences are studied, where grade 6 took a stand on one issue. Assessed by a presented study, a written comparison and a perspective contrast, scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"da","8","da-g8-connect","DA:Cn10.1.8a; DA:Cn10.1.8b; DA:Cn11.1.8a","da-g7-connect","da-g9-connect","like-me-line; dance-two-ties; research-notes","like-me-line; dance-two-ties; research-notes","link; perspective; two topics; choreograph; research; eras","","Link dances to each other and say how they shape the student's view, research two contrasting topics and choreograph both, and analyze what dances from many cultures and eras show about their people's ideas. What changes: two topics and many eras are held together, where grade 7 studied one style's history. Assessed by two choreographed pieces, a written analysis and a linking item, scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."

