320: | 4 | 6 | Dance with a point of view. *How can a dance take a stand on an issue, and what does a dance's movement mean to the people who made it?* | 14 | da-g6-stage; da-g6-connect | DA:Pr6.1.6a; DA:Pr6.1.6b; DA:Cn10.1.6a; DA:Cn10.1.6b; DA:Cn11.1.6a | Performing (Pr6); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Kurt Jooss, The Green Table (Germany, 1932; Jooss died 1979), a dance against war set around a table of talking diplomats, described and linked to a ballet company's or a dance archive's page; figurines of dancers from Tang dynasty tombs (China, 7th to 8th century, a museum's open-access collection; the unit plan picks the figurine and records its page), with the whirling dance that reached China along the Silk Roads from Sogdiana in Central Asia, studied by looking only; Santee Smith, Kaha:wi (Kanien'keh�:ka, Six Nations of the Grand River, Ontario, Canada, 2004; Kaha:wi Dance Theatre; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a dance of family and generations, described and linked to the company's page, watched only | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 5 one dance adapted to two spaces (da-g5-stage) and a dance from another subject's topic (da-g5-connect), each with its reteach. DA:Pr6.1.6a: adapt the moves to the performing space, practice performance manners (entering, holding stillness, the bow, being an audience) and apply the choreographer's notes. DA:Pr6.1.6b: compare production elements (the room's lights on or off or daylight, plain clothing colors, the class's own sound, a light prop), choose some and explain the choices in production terms. DA:Cn10.1.6a: compare the qualities of one style studied with the student's own movement tastes in the log, or with an invented dancer's, marked the same. DA:Cn10.1.6b: research a social issue from the class list (shared spaces and litter, water, being left out, noise and quiet) on the research card, make a group dance that takes a viewpoint on it, and discuss