299:| 6 | 6 | Dances in many styles. *How do dances from different places use counts and phrases, and how does expressing myself change how much I join in?* | 14 | g6-dance-sequences; g6-express-variety | SHAPE.S1.8.2; SHAPE.S4.8.1; SHAPE.S4.8.3 | Cues: "count, level, transition". Settings: a line dance, a partner social dance, a group's own rhythmic routine | Movement sequences in a range of dance styles: eight-count phrases, transitions and a sequence of four phrases (S1.8.2). Picks: cumbia's basic step, a social partner dance from Colombia's Caribbean coast, taught as its basic side and back steps in eight counts with teacher-set partners, no gender roles and a hand hold or side by side; a four-wall line dance in eight counts of our own, with no cultural claim; and a group's own rhythmic routine. Explain how self-expression affects how involved a person gets (S4.8.1), and take part in many kinds of activity that improve health, recording the kinds taken part in this year (units 2 and 3 fitness, units 4 and 5 team games, this unit's dances) and saying which fits best (S4.8.3, in full); goal-cycle and calm-steps first used here (a goal for the routine; a calm step before performing for a small group). Opens with a retrieval task on grade 5's gym and dance sequence and review of experiences. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: a four-phrase sequence to the beat, the baseline cue "transition"; knowledge task: one way self-expression changed how a person joined in, and the kind of activity that fits best and why; conduct: welcomes a partner's ideas in the group routine, S3.8.1 review), 1 respond day. Tools: music on the teacher's device or the teacher's count; dance cards and each dance's source line (Curio Class writes them) | Dance needs a year of movement ideas (unit 5) for levels and transitions; S4 sits here because a performance is where self-expression shows, and the record looks back on the year's kinds of activity | Candidate: groups teach each other one phrase |