306:| 13 | 7 | Change the dance. *How can space, time, force and relationship change a dance, and how do friends and choices keep people joining in?* | 13 | g7-dance-ideas; g7-people-interests; g7-challenges | SHAPE.S2.8.8; SHAPE.S4.8.2; SHAPE.S4.8.4; SHAPE.S4.8.5 | Cues: "same steps, new idea: unison, canon, contrast". Settings: grade 6's line dance and a circle dance changed by groups, a rhythm form with sticks or claps | Use movement ideas (space, time, force, relationship) to change different dance and rhythm forms: a unison section, a canon, a contrast, in a line dance, a circle dance and a rhythm form (S2.8.8). Pick: kolo, a Serbian chain dance danced in a circle at community gatherings (on UNESCO's intangible heritage list since 2017), taught in a simple step pattern, then changed with canon and contrast. Explain how time with others affects involvement (S4.8.2) and link choice and interests to how engaged a person becomes (S4.8.4): groups choose their dance style and roles; closely examine challenges faced alone and as a group in movement (fear of failing, space, a fitness gap) and test one fix in rehearsal (S4.8.5). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 6's sequences and self-expression and unit 5's movement ideas. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: perform a sequence changed with one idea, the baseline cue "canon"; knowledge tasks: say which idea changed it; say how working with others changed an invented dancer's joining in; connect a choice to staying involved; name a challenge with a fix and say how the fix went; conduct: includes every group member's idea, S3.8.2 review), 1 respond day. Tools: music on the teacher's device or claps; rhythm sticks (rolled paper on the low-equipment path) | Dance returns each year (units 6, 13, 18, 22) so it is never taught once and dropped; involvement and challenges sit with group choreography, where choice and others shape joining in | Candidate: a canon the class performs in two halves | Cultural content: Serbian community dance tradition; taught with respect, no costume; no sacred use. Spot-checked 2026-11-25: UNESCO's Representative List entry "Kolo, traditional folk dance" (https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/kolo-traditional-folk-dance-01270) describes a social chain dance that involves the whole community; the Serbian national heritage register's entry on kolo (https://nkns.rs/cyr/popis-nkns/kolo-kolo-u-tri-kolo-u-shest, opened by the second-model read 2026-11-25) gives its context, and the agent confirms the simple step pattern at it first; the students' canon and contrast are their own choreography, not the tradition. Challenges: fear of failing named kindly; no student's challenge is shared without consent. Swap allowed. Cost: $0 |
