tails, and an objective summary with no "I liked" (RL.6.2); the plot episode by episode with a character-change line under the story map (RL.6.3). The Yu retelling read beside the novella for how each hero meets the river's trials (grade 5's theme from how characters respond, in review). The discussion routine taught with frames: come having read and marked the text (SL.6.1a), agreed rules, goals and deadlines, roles (SL.6.1b), questions answered with elaboration (SL.6.1c), key ideas reviewed and others' views paraphrased (SL.6.1d). The daily evidence paragraph: point, quoted line, explanation, on the novella (W.6.9a) and on the explainers (W.6.9b's first use; tracing an argument comes in unit 2), short and long (W.6.10). The band named; the oral reading check and the reading log set up (RL.6.10, RI.6.10). A story in which a character makes a choice the river forces on them, through the full process: orient the reader with narrator and characters (W.6.3a), dialogue, pacing and description (W.6.3b), transitions that signal a shift in time or setting (W.6.3c), precise and sensory words (W.6.3d), an ending that follows (W.6.3e); planned against task, purpose and audience (W.6.4); peer and teacher feedback, revision and editing (W.6.5); sentence patterns varied by combining sentences from the novella and the draft (L.6.3a) and one style and tone kept (L.6.3b); pronouns in their own drafts: case (L.6.1a), intensive pronouns (L.6.1b), shifts in number and person (L.6.1c), vague antecedents (L.6.1d), and variations from standard English identified in their own and others' writing and speech (the novella's dialogue, a recorded exchange), which form fits a named setting explained, and a model, reference or checklist used to revise chosen examples into conventional English (e); typed, about two pages (grade 5's end, unscored retrieval), with peer comments (W.6.6 begins) | A long shared book first, so citing, summary, discussion and the evidence paragraph are taught on one text everyone has read. It picks up grade 5's water unit as story (too much water, a town sharing the cost), and carries Cory's heroes-and-trials decision (a present-day hero, a myth retold as the pair). Grade 5's unit 1 began a family over in a new place; this one stays and chooses. Pronouns sit in the first process piece's revise days, as the row asks. First-use retrieval in the opening lessons: quoting accurately and theme from responses (e5-quote-accurately, e5-theme-response, e5-compare-characters), grade 5 spring reading records with missing/flagged follow-up (e5-reading-range, e5-fluency), discussion roles (e5-discussion), and a one-paragraph narrative sample with its revision (e5-narrative, e5-writing-tasks, e5-writing-process, e5-grammar). A word list is kept from the novella and the explainers as grade 5's e5-academic-words in use | Candidate: the text record for the novella (RUBRIC-ELA 2b, four headings of what makes it hard); the discussion look-for sheet (2i); an end task joining reading and writing on the theme with scoring guidance (2v) | A flooded home, money worries and a hard choice about moving: students who have lived through a disaster may be in the room, so no student is asked to share their own; no death on the page. The Yu retelling is read as a story from a living tradition, not as quaint, and gets the sensitivity agent's read after it studies Chinese sources on the tale (brief). L.6.1e: home languages and dialects are respected and never corrected as wrong in themselves. The novella gets the text agent's read and the sensitivity read |
