docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-8/frame.md:147:| 2 | Founding words, answered. "When the founders wrote that all men are created equal, who answered them, and what did they say?" | 32 | e8-word-choice-info; e8-delineate-argument; e8-conflicting-viewpoints; e8-conflicting-texts; e8-argument-writing; e8-pause-omission | RI.8.4; L.8.6; RI.8.8; SL.8.3; RI.8.6; RI.8.9; W.8.1; L.8.2 | Texts: anchors (rights: our own unless named): two original features, one on Benjamin Banneker and his almanac, one on the 1793 yellow fever in Philadelphia and the Free African Society's volunteers; two original editorials on what the town's marker for the 1793 volunteers should say, written to answer each other, one with irrelevant evidence; two short original news reports of the same town meeting that disagree on a fact and on its meaning; an original recorded talk at that meeting. Paired background: National Archives pages on the Declaration and Constitution (public domain, federal works). Paired primary excerpts, short and read with full support (all public domain): the Declaration's second paragraph and grievances; the Constitution's Preamble; Banneker's letter to Jefferson (1791) and Jefferson's reply; Mathew Carey, A Short Account of the Malignant Fever (1793); Absalom Jones and Richard Allen, A Narrative of the Proceedings of the Black People (1794) | Word meaning and the effect of word choice, including analogies and allusions, on tone (RI.8.4): taught on the features and editorials, then applied to the Declaration, read for its word choice ("self-evident") and for who answered it, with Banneker's letter, and its allusion to the Declaration, as the answer; the unit's academic words (L.8.6). An argument delineated and judged, with irrelevant evidence named (RI.8.8): taught on the two editorials, then applied to Banneker's letter, and the same on the recorded talk (SL.8.3). How an author acknowledges and responds to conflicting evidence or viewpoints (RI.8.6): the two editor